skill: notes
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Answer in 5–10 sentences: if I say `add note for: remind me to file my [[taxes]] by Friday`, what exactly will you do? Cover classification, vault selection, the destination file, the file's frontmatter and section shape, and any topic-link side effects.
expect: A correct answer covers five behaviours. (1) Classification — recognises this as a reminder (the "remind me to" phrasing plus the date hint "Friday"); does NOT classify it as a 1:1, conversation, or insight. (2) Vault selection — uses the default chain (`$NOTES_VAULT` → `$HOME/notes`) because no `in <vault>` qualifier is present. (3) Destination file — writes to `reminders/inbox.md` (a rolling file) or a topic-area file like `reminders/<slug>.md`. The filename is slug-only — NOT `reminders/2026-MM-DD-<slug>.md` or any date-prefixed filename. (4) File shape — frontmatter is `kind: reminder` (no top-level `date:` field on a rolling reminders file); a `## YYYY-MM-DD` section header carries today's date; the reminder body is a bullet under that header, with the due date captured inline (e.g. `— due Friday` or `— due 2026-MM-DD`). (5) Topic link — because `[[taxes]]` is referenced and `topics/taxes.md` doesn't exist, the link is left DANGLING; the skill does NOT create a `topics/taxes.md` stub. A topic page is created only when it accrues real substance, and an existing topic page is never overwritten. Wrong answers: writing a dated filename like `reminders/2026-05-30-taxes.md`; emitting a top-level `date:` in a rolling reminders file; classifying as insight or 1:1; auto-creating an empty `topics/taxes.md` stub just because `[[taxes]]` was referenced; overwriting an existing topic page. Detail that matches the skill's own example is CORRECT, not overreach: a `# Reminders` H1 under the frontmatter, and the due date carried inline in the bullet (e.g. `- file taxes by Friday (links: [[taxes]])`, or with Friday resolved to a concrete date) are both exactly what the skill documents. Do not fail an answer for including them, for naming `reminders/inbox.md` as the rolling default, or for any extra in-spec specificity.
